# Day 02 — Explaining a Bug

**Trigger:** you say **"Start english day 2"** / **"Bắt đầu english ngày 2"** — cross-check `english-playbooks.mdc` Section 1 (Day 2 row) + `english-context.mdc` (Coaching approach + Strict mode).

**Today's linker focus:** Group 5 — **Examples / Explanation**. Full reference: `english-linkers.mdc`.

**Daily Focus:** explain a bug in **90 seconds or less** with a clear structure — **what broke · root cause · fix · impact**. This is the single most common technical conversation a senior dev has (standups, on-call, PR descriptions, incident channels). Win this, and you sound senior in any dev room.

---

## 1. Core Knowledge (10 min read)

### 1.1 Key phrases (10) — bug explanation in dev context

| # | Phrase | When to use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | **The bug was that [X] when [Y].** | Open with the symptom |
| 2 | **I tracked it down to [Z].** | State root cause cleanly |
| 3 | **It turned out to be a [caching / race / null] issue.** | Summarize the type of bug |
| 4 | **The fix is straightforward — [one-line change].** | Describe a small fix |
| 5 | **The real fix needs [refactor / coordination] — this is a patch for now.** | Senior honesty — patch vs proper fix |
| 6 | **Impact: [N users / which flow / what broke].** | Quantify severity |
| 7 | **We saw it on [iOS / Android / prod] only.** | Scope the bug |
| 8 | **I'm not 100% sure yet — still investigating.** | Hedge when you don't know the cause |
| 9 | **I was able to reproduce it locally.** | Confirm you own the bug |
| 10 | **I added a regression test so it won't come back.** | Close with prevention |

> Memorize phrases **1, 2, 4, 6** today. Those four cover a standup-length bug explanation.

### 1.2 Linking words — Group 5 (Examples / Explanation) — TODAY'S FOCUS

Pulled from `english-linkers.mdc` — Group 5. Use these to **ground abstract claims in concrete examples**. This is the number one senior habit in technical communication.

| Phrase | Register | Dev example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **For example / For instance** | neutral — most common | *"The state wasn't resetting — **for example,** after logout, the old user's name still showed on the profile."* |
| **Such as** | listing items | *"Errors **such as** 401 and 403 both trigger a logout in our client."* |
| **Like** | very US, casual | *"Frameworks **like** React Native and Flutter handle gestures differently."* |
| **In other words** | clarify / rephrase | *"The token was saved but never read — **in other words,** the app was logged-in in storage but not in memory."* |

**Senior habit:** never leave an abstract statement without an example. If you say *"We had a caching issue"*, immediately say *"**For example,** the profile picture stayed on the old user after switching accounts."*

**Plus 1 linker from Day 1 for sequence** — you already know it: **First… Then… After that…** (Group 6). Keep using it when you walk through debug steps.

### 1.3 Thinking phrases (3) — buy 2–3 seconds mid-explanation

- **Give me a second — let me pull up the ticket.**
- **Actually, let me rephrase that.**
- **Hmm, the short answer is [X]. The long answer — [Y].**

> The third one is a senior trick. It signals: "I have a quick answer, but I'm also ready to go deep if you want." Sounds confident either way.

### 1.4 Softening sentences (3) — uncertain about the cause

- **I could be wrong, but it looks like a timing issue.**
- **I haven't fully confirmed it yet — my best guess is the cache.**
- **This is still a hypothesis, but [X] would explain the behavior.**

> Senior devs hedge on root cause until they have **evidence**. Saying *"It's definitely a cache bug"* before you've proved it is how junior devs lose credibility.

### 1.5 Structured answer templates (2)

**Template A — S-R-F-I (Symptom · Root cause · Fix · Impact)** — best for standup / incident

> **Symptom:** What the user / system observed.
> **Root cause:** Why it happened (one sentence).
> **Fix:** What you changed.
> **Impact:** Who was affected and how bad.

**Template B — R-I-F (Repro · Investigation · Fix)** — best for PR description / code review

> **Repro:** Steps to reproduce, or "happens intermittently on [platform]".
> **Investigation:** What you checked and what you ruled out — end with the root cause.
> **Fix:** The change and why this is the right fix (or why it's a patch).

> **Use A** when someone asks "What happened?" at standup — 30-60 seconds.
> **Use B** when writing a PR — 3-6 bullet points. Same structure, different medium.

