## 3. Speaking Practice — MANDATORY (15 min)

### A. Shadowing — say each sentence aloud 3× (match rhythm, not just words)

1. The bug was that users got logged out right after signing in.
2. I tracked it down to a timing issue in SecureStore.
3. It turned out to be a race condition between the cache and the refetch.
4. For example, the profile picture stayed on the old user after logout.
5. I added a regression test so it won't come back.

> **Rule:** target ~3 seconds per sentence. Bug explanations at standup are **fast** — no slow-motion.

### B. Build your own answer — 30 seconds each

1. **"Can you explain the last bug you fixed?"** → use **Template A (S-R-F-I)**. Pick a real bug from your Day 4 or Day 5 work. Record or type.
2. **"What's the status on the login issue?"** → use a **softening pattern** from 1.4 if you're still investigating. Include **one example** ("for example…"). Record or type.

### C. Real scenario simulation — 90 seconds

> You're on a 10-person engineering standup. Yesterday you fixed the 401 → logout bug from Day 5 of your coding track. The EM asks you to give a quick rundown — what was the bug, what fixed it, any impact. Give the answer now.
>
> **Rules:**
> - Use **Template A (S-R-F-I)** structure.
> - Use **at least 2 key phrases** from 1.1 (phrases 1–6).
> - Use **at least 1 linker from Group 5** (for example / such as / in other words).
> - Use **one sequence linker** from Day 1 (first / then / after that).
> - Record yourself or paste the script to AI for review.

---

## 4. Fast Response Training — ≤ 5 seconds each (10 min)

Speed matters more than perfection. If you hesitate, start with a thinking phrase ("Give me a second — let me pull up the ticket…"), then answer.

1. **"Is the login bug fixed?"**
   → *"Yeah — fix is up for review."* **or** *"Almost — still running QA."*
2. **"What caused it?"**
   → *"Turned out to be a SecureStore timing issue — token saved but state not updated."*
3. **"Any impact?"**
   → *"Hit every user on the latest build. Hotfix this morning, error rate dropped to zero."* **or** *"Cosmetic only, no user reports."*

> **Drill:** set a 5-second timer. Answer before it beeps. Repeat each question 3×.

---

## 5. Thinking in English — pattern, not translation

The trap: Vietnamese devs often describe bugs in a **flat, listy** way. English seniors **structure** the explanation (symptom first, cause second, fix third). Rewire the pattern — don't translate word-by-word.

| VN thought | ❌ Word-by-word | ✅ Native dev pattern |
| --- | --- | --- |
| *"Cái bug là do token lưu vào SecureStore nhưng state chưa update nên app tự logout."* | *"The bug is because token save to SecureStore but state not update yet so app auto logout."* | *"The bug was that users got logged out right after login. **I tracked it down to** a timing issue — we saved the token but didn't update the auth state in the same call."* |
| *"Chưa chắc nguyên nhân, có thể do race condition."* | *"Not sure reason yet, maybe because race condition."* | *"**I'm not 100% sure yet — my best guess is** a race condition between the cache and the refetch."* |
| *"Ảnh hưởng hết user trên bản mới, đã fix hotfix sáng nay."* | *"Affect all user new version, already fix hotfix this morning."* | *"**Impact:** every user on the latest build. **Rolled out as a hotfix** this morning — error rate dropped to zero."* |

**Pattern takeaways:**

- VN describes **flat** ("A because B so C"). English structures ("**Symptom** — I tracked it down to **cause** — **fix**").
- VN *"chưa chắc"* → English senior phrase: *"I'm not 100% sure yet — my best guess is X."* (hedge + best guess, not just *"not sure"*).
- VN *"ảnh hưởng hết user"* → English **quantifies**: *"every user on the latest build"*, *"about 5% of iOS 17 users"*, *"no reported impact"*. Senior devs give **numbers** whenever possible.
- VN drops subjects; English keeps them (*"I tracked it down"*, not *"Tracked it down"*).
- VN rarely gives examples mid-explanation. English does — that's why Group 5 linkers matter.

